## 📌 Overview

This project builds and evaluates **ten machine learning models** for predicting lung cancer occurrence using demographic data, lifestyle habits (smoking, alcohol consumption), and clinical symptoms. We compare model performance on both the original Kaggle dataset and a synthetic dataset generated using **Gretel AI's ActGAN** model.

**Key Result:** Random Forest achieved the highest AUC of **0.9919**, followed by XGBoost and Logistic Regression, demonstrating strong discriminatory power for lung cancer prediction.

---

## 📊 Dataset

| Property | Details |
|----------|---------|
| **Source** | [Lung Cancer Dataset — Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/mysarahmadbhat/lung-cancer) |
| **Instances** | 309 (original) + synthetic data via [Gretel AI](https://console.gretel.ai/) |
| **Attributes** | 16 (15 predictive + 1 class label) |
| **Features** | Gender, Age, Smoking, Yellow Fingers, Anxiety, Peer Pressure, Chronic Disease, Fatigue, Allergy, Wheezing, Alcohol, Coughing, Shortness of Breath, Swallowing Difficulty, Chest Pain |
| **Target** | Lung Cancer (Yes / No) |

### Synthetic Data Generation

A synthetic dataset was generated using **Gretel AI's ActGAN** model to augment the original data. Configuration included privacy filters for outlier handling, automatic batch sizing, and conditional vector generation. The synthetic data maintained high correlation with the original dataset.

